Debouncing is basically saying 'wait until they stop, then do the thing.' Perfect for search inputs or resize handlers where you don't want to fire on every keystroke.

**How it works:**
- Timer starts on first call
- Each new call resets the timer
- Function only runs when timer completes

**Implementation:**
```javascript
function debounce(fn, delay) {
  let timeoutId;
  return function(...args) {
    clearTimeout(timeoutId);
    timeoutId = setTimeout(() => fn.apply(this, args), delay);
  };
}
```

**How I use it:**
```javascript
const debouncedSearch = debounce(handleSearch, 300);
input.addEventListener('input', debouncedSearch);
```

I use this all the time for search bars — waits 300ms after typing stops before hitting the API. Saves tons of unnecessary requests.
